1.) cd licensing-service
    mvnd spring-boot:run "-Dspring-boot.run.arguments=--server.port=8081"

2.) http://localhost:8080/v1/organization/optimaGrowth/license/0235431845

3.) http://localhost:8080/v1/organization/optimaGrowth/license

4.) Putting everything together
    Here is the architecture to remember:
    When a client sends a request to your licensing service:
    HTTP and routing: The controller receives the request.
    Business logic: The service processes the operation.
    Data representation: The model represents the license.
    API response: The controller returns the result, potentially including HATEOAS links.
    Internationalisation: If configured, the application 
    resolves messages in the appropriate language.
    Future improvements: Database persistence, validation, security and error handling 
    make the service more robust.
    The five key takeaways
    Separation of concerns: Controllers handle HTTP, services handle business logic, 
    and models represent data.
    Resource-oriented URLs: /v1/organization/{organizationId}/license communicates 
    both API version and resource ownership.
    Internationalisation: Externalised messages make it easier to support multiple languages.
    HATEOAS: Hypermedia links help clients discover related API operations.
    Incremental development: Starting without a database or comprehensive error handling 
    lets you learn the architecture before adding production complexity.
    The central lesson: You're not simply building an endpoint that creates licenses. 
    You're learning how to structure a microservice so that it can grow to support persistence,
    security, internationalisation and more sophisticated API behaviour without everything
    becoming tangled together.